# ffi/logs/rust/stage2-four-arms-M1.log
# W3 stage 2: four arms over M1, payloads P1.1, P1.2 and P1.3.
# 2026-09-18
#
# CONFIGURATION (R7)
#   machine        4 vCPU Intel Xeon @ 2.80GHz, 15 GB, Ubuntu 24.04.4, Linux 6.18.44 x86_64,
#                  container, NOT an isolated host. No CPU pinning, no governor control.
#   rust           rustc/cargo 1.94.1, profile release (opt-level 3, lto off, codegen-units 16)
#   MSRV declared  1.88. NOT VERIFIED: no 1.88 toolchain exists in this container.
#   incumbent      prost 0.14.4 (prost-build 0.14.4, protox 0.9.1; no protoc here)
#   binding        a cdylib (libak_core.so) resolved by the dynamic linker. Section 4 of
#                  this log shows the ABI entry points are undefined imports, so the host
#                  cannot inline them. A C or C++ host that STATICALLY links the same core
#                  gets a direct call instead, which is cheaper; this slice measures the
#                  shared-library case, which is what every managed host does.
#   guard          ON for sections 3 and 5 (ABI v1 section 5). Section 6 is guard OFF.
#   content set    ascii (design/SHAPES.md). latin1 and wide are NOT measured.
#   reference      ffi/schema/generated/manifest.json, VALIDATED in stage 1
#
# WHAT EACH ARM'S BYTE IDENTITY IS CHECKED AGAINST
#   prost          prost-build structs, objects built by the hand-written builder of
#                  crates/stage1-validate. Independent of everything else here.
#   armonik        facade types, objects built by the GENERATED builder, encoded by
#                  GENERATED prost::Message impls. Independent of the prost arm on both
#                  the object graph and the codec.
#   core-native    the same facade values, the generated core traversal, no boundary.
#   core-ffi-rust  the same facade values, THE SAME core traversal, through the C ABI.
#                  NOT independent of core-native: they share the codec, so neither
#                  validates the other. What their agreement proves is the BINDING.
#   All four are checked against the manifest, which a third implementation
#   (prost-reflect) validated in stage 1. That is where the independence comes from.
#
# HAZARDS (R9)
#   - 4 shared vCPUs in a container. Absolutes do not travel; the ratios are formed
#     inside one process from rounds interleaved across the arms (R4).
#   - Section 6 (guard off) is a DIFFERENT PROCESS from section 5. prost is carried in
#     both as the in-process control, and only the guard-on-against-guard-off ratio of
#     one arm to prost in its own process is a fair comparison.
#   - The counting build (section 3) is a different build from the timed one. No timing
#     in this log comes from it, and the timed build has the counters compiled out.
#   - P1.1 is 858 bytes: at ~600 ns a round is dominated by fixed per-call cost, so its
#     ratios are a call-rate figure and not a throughput figure.
#   - ASCII only. On the string path that is the cheapest content set for every arm.
#   - core-ffi-rust decode allocates a fresh facade object graph per call, as do the
#     other three. Nothing here reuses a decoded object.

